          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The term
            %strong tensor product
            has many different but closely related meanings.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In its original sense a
                %em tensor product
                is a representing object for a suitable sort of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilinear+map"} bilinear map
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multilinear+map"} multilinear map
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+spaces"} vector spaces
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                over a
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                over a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+over+a+monad"} algebras
                over a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+monad"} commutative monad
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            %li
              %p
                Consequently, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes : C \times C \to C
                which is part of the data of any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                is also often called a
                = succeed "," do
                  %em tensor product
                %em any
                monoidal category underlies a multicategory in a canonical way). In parts of the literature (certain)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian
                monoidal categories are even addressed as
                = succeed "." do
                  %em tensor categories
            %li
              %p
                Given two objects in a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C,\otimes)
                with a right and left
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                %em
                  tensor product over
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is the quotient of their tensor product in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                by this action. If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is commutative, then this is a special case of the tensor product in a multicategory.
            %li
              %p
                This generalizes to modules over
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monads
                in a bicategory, which includes the notion of
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+functors"} tensor product of functors
            %li
              %p
                Finally, tensor products in a multicategory and tensor products over monads in a bicategory are both special cases of tensor products in a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#in_a_multicategory In a multicategory
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              %strong tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
              is defined to be an object equipped with a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+construction"} universal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multimorphism"} multimorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to A \otimes B
              in that any multimorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to C
              factors uniquely through
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to A \otimes B
              via a (1-ary) morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi B
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B\to C
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is the category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
              of abelian groups, made into a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
              using
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multilinear+maps"} multilinear maps
              as the multimorphisms, then we get the usual
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
              is equipped with a universal map from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times B
              (as a set) to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              such that this map is linear (a group homomorphism) in each argument separately. This tensor product can also be constructed explicitly by
            %ol
              %li
                starting with the cartesian product
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
                in sets,
              %li
                generating a
                %em free
                abelian group from it, and then
              %li
                quotienting by relations
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a_1,b)+(a_2,b)\sim (a_1+a_2,b)
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∼
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 1
                        %mo +
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,b_1)+(a,b_2)\sim (a,b_1+b_2)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,b)\sim 0
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a,0)\sim 0
                follow automatically; you need them explicitly if you generalise to
                = succeed "s.)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+monoid"} abelian monoid
          %p
            Note that in this case,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes B
            is not a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject"} subobject
            %em or
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ×
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times B
            %em can
            be obtained as a quotient of some other pre-existing product; see
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            below.
          %p Other examples of tensor products in multicategories:
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-categories
              is a tensor product in the multicategory of 2-categories and
              = succeed "s." do
                %span.newWikiWord
                  cubical functor
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/cubical+functor"} ?
          .num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              In particular, any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed category
              (even if not monoidal) has an underlying multicategory. Tensor products in this multicategory are characterized by the adjointness relation
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(A\otimes B, C) \cong \hom(A, \hom(B,C)).
            %p This may be the oldest notion of tensor product, since the definition of the internal-hom of abelian groups and vector spaces, unlike that of their tensor product, is intuitively obvious.
          %p
            While the universal property referred to above (every bilinear map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to C
            factors uniquely through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to A\otimes B
            via a map
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes B \to C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi D
                    %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi E
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_1,\dots,D_m,A,B,E_1,\dots, E_n \to C
            factors uniquely through
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B\to A\otimes B
            via a multilinear map
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi D
                      %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D_1,\dots,D_m, A\otimes B ,E_1,\dots, E_n \to C
          %h3#in_terms_of_heteromorphisms In terms of heteromorphisms
          %p
            An alternative approach is to define the tensor product via an inter-categorical universal property involving
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heteromorphism"} heteromorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi het
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom (a \otimes b, c) \simeq het (\langle a, b \rangle, c)
            in general. That is to say, any morphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \otimes b
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            in some category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            corresponds to a heteromorphism from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle a, b \rangle
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \times C
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi het
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} het (\langle a, b \rangle, c)
          %p
            When tensor products exist, we have a canonical het
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi η
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mo →
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta_{\langle a, b \rangle} \colon \langle a, b \rangle \to a \otimes b
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_{a \otimes b} \in hom (a \otimes b, a \otimes b)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi \colon \langle a, b \rangle \to c
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mtable{:columnalign => "center center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi a
                          %mo ,
                          %mi b
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                        %msub
                          %mi η
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi a
                            %mo ,
                            %mi b
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                      %mtd
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtd
                        %mover
                          %mo ↘
                          %mi ϕ
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                      %mtd
                        %mi a
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi b
                      %mtd
                        %munder
                          %mo ⤏
                          %mi f
                      %mtd
                        %mi c
                      %mtd
                    %mtr
                      %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{array}{cccC}
                  \&amp; {\langle a, b \rangle} &amp;  &amp;   &amp; \\
                  \\eta_{\langle a, b \rangle} &amp; \downarrow           &amp; \overset{\phi}\searrow       &amp;     &amp; \\
                  \&amp; a \otimes b            &amp; \underset{f}\dashrightarrow &amp; c &amp;  \\ \end{array}
          %p This represents an example of a more general method for translating universal properties in multicategories into ones involving heteromorphisms.
          %h3#of_modules_in_a_monoidal_category Of modules in a monoidal category
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            and consider the multicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mod"} Mod
            of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multilinear+maps"} multilinear maps
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes_R B
            of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            can be constructed as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes B
            underlying them by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi B
                  %mo =
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi r
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∼
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi r
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes_R B = A\otimes B / (a,r\cdot b) \sim (a\cdot r,b).
          %p
            More category-theoretically, this can be constructed as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
            of the two maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi R
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⇉
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes R \otimes B \;\rightrightarrows\; A\otimes B
          %p
            given by the action of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            This tensor product can be generalized to the case when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is not commutative, as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a right
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            is a left
            = succeed "-module." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            (a ring being a monoid in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            with its tensor product), we can define the tensor product of a left and a right
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a commutative monoid in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes_R B
            is again an
            = succeed "-module," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is not commutative then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi R
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\otimes_R B
            will no longer be an
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Not all tensor products in multicategories are instances of this construction. In particular, the tensor product in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                is not the tensor product of modules over any monoid in the cartesian monoidal category
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                %em can
                be considered as “sets with an action by something,” but that something is more complicated than a monoid: it is a special sort of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
                called a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+theory"} commutative theory
            %li
              %p
                Conversely, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is a
                %em commutative
                monoid in a symmetric monoidal category, there is a multicategory of
                = succeed "-modules" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                is not commutative this is impossible. However, see the section on tensor products in virtual double categories, below.
          %h3#of_modules_in_a_bicategory Of modules in a bicategory
          %p
            The tensor product of left and right modules over a noncommutative monoid in a monoidal category is a special case of the tensor product of modules for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: x\to x
            is a monad in a bicategory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A: y\to x
            with an action by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B: x\to z
            with an action by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\to z
            given by a similar coequalizer. Regarding a monoidal category as a 1-object bicategory, this recovers the above definition.
          %p
            For example, consider the bicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Mat
            of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrices
            for some monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Mat
            is a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "-bimodule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi I
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A,I)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to V
            = succeed "-bimodule" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (I,A)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}\to V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Mat
            is a classical construction called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+functors"} tensor product of functors
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
          %h3#in_a_virtual_double_category In a virtual double category
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/virtual+double+category"} virtual double category
            is a common generalization of a multicategory and a bicategory (and actually of a
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
          %p On the other hand, in any bicategory (in fact, any double category) there is a virtual double category whose objects are monads and whose 1-cells are bimodules, and the tensor product in this virtual double category is the tensor product of modules in a bicategory defined above. Thus, tensor products in a virtual double category include all notions of tensor product discussed above.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+groups"} tensor product of groups
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+modules"} tensor product of modules
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+spaces"} tensor product of vector spaces
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+tensor+product"} inductive tensor product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smash+product"} smash product
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+sets"} pointed sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+representations"} tensor product of representations
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+vector+bundles"} tensor product of vector bundles
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras"} tensor product of algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+algebras+over+a+commutative+monad"} tensor product of algebras over a commutative monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Lie+algebras"} tensor product of Lie algebras
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Banach+spaces"} tensor product of Banach spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+product"} balanced product
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spaces"} G-spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+smash+product"} balanced smash product
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+space"} pointed
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spaces"} G-spaces
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+functors"} tensor product of functors
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+product+category"} tensor product of enriched categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+tensor+product+of+abelian+categories"} Deligne tensor product of abelian categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-categories"} tensor product of presentable (infinity,1)-categories
            %li
              %p
                in terms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+type+theory"} linear type theory
                the tensor product is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
                of the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"} multiplicative conjunction
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+calculus"} tensor calculus
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/external+tensor+product"} external tensor product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cotensor+product"} cotensor product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout+product"} pushout product
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multiplicative+conjunction"}> multiplicative conjunction
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+entanglement"} quantum entanglement
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composite+system"} composite system
          %div
            %table
              %thead
                %tr
                  %th
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+%28as+an+operation%29"} homotopy
                  %th
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
